thbf{r}(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262673 :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=15\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+7\\operatorn